\section*{III.C Projection Geometry \& Cosmological Unfolding (PGCU)}

\subsection*{III.C.1 Geometric Construction: $\Sigma_t$, $\theta_4$, and $\rho_{\text{link}}$}

In this section, we develop a fully geometric interpretation of cosmological expansion within the SAT framework. This approach is centered on the behavior of the time surface $\Sigma_t$ as it expands radially through a structured filament manifold. The resulting geometry gives rise to an emergent expansion behavior that naturally reproduces key features of standard cosmology without invoking external mechanisms such as a cosmological constant or inflaton field.

The central object is the time surface $\Sigma_t$, defined as a three-dimensional topological front of resolution expanding outward from the origin. This surface interacts with a distribution of radially oriented topological filaments $\gamma_i(\lambda)$, each carrying intrinsic SAT parameters $\theta_4$, $\tau$, and $Q$. The geometry of their intersection is governed by two functions:

\begin{itemize}
  \item $\theta_4(r)$: the average angle between the local normal to $\Sigma_t$ and the filament at radius $r$
  \item $\rho_{\text{link}}(r)$: the density of filament intersections with $\Sigma_t$ per unit area at radius $r$
\end{itemize}

These parameters define the \emph{resistance to resolution} experienced by $\Sigma_t$ as it sweeps through the filament field.

